      "id": 69057,
      "name": "Foote",
      "username": "Foote",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/foote/{size}/1877_2.png",
      "created_at": "2013-08-01T22:23:42.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"jagarciao\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/jagarciao/48/2010_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> jagarciao:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>But it would also be logical and within reason to have the H reduced since H is defined in the rules as the \"current\" number of players… but we have been told that H is, in fact, not reduced when a player leaves the game for some reason.&nbsp;So, while logic is indeed on your side, we can't really rely on logic to dictate rulings anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;I have to admit that all this ambiguous wordings and sometimes counterintuitive or even contradictory situations has been taking away from the game a little bit for me. I do love the game and have a group of people I play with where we don't have a problem because we have such a laid attitude about how things work and we tweak them freely as necessary… but I have often had a hard time explaining (or defending) certain card interactions when trying to bring the game to new players… to the point that I tend to avoid it and just stick to playing with my group only.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Yeah I understand that totally. Just the other day I was playing with a few new people to the game and took slight issue with one of the interactions (I don't remember which), but made a pretty decent thematic argument for it so I allowed it. At the end of day, you still just gotta have fun with it.&nbsp;</p>",
